   "source": [
    "# Case Study 1 — MSigDB-guided component selection and BoNesis optimization\n",
    "\n",
    "**Complete pipeline:** load/standardize the original Nestorowa S0–S5 observations → load the original DoRothEA A–C network → **download the entire Mouse MSigDB** → find gene sets containing hematopoiesis-related words in their **names** → extract member genes and rank them → intersect with DoRothEA → select biological seeds → expand through regulatory neighbors and short bridges → prepare a smaller GRN → run the **original** BoNesis component-selection constraints and objectives.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is a *modified*, seed-guided version of Chevalier et al. Case Study 1, **not an exact replication of their unrestricted component selection**. No published 39-gene list is used anywhere to select seeds.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Requirements\n",
    "\n",
    "- Run Jupyter from your project folder so relative paths such as `data/...` resolve.\n",
    "- Keep `gene_name_standardization.py` alongside this notebook.\n",
    "- The three original data files must be available:\n",
    "  - `data/nestorowa_singlecell_binarized_observations.csv`\n",
    "  - `data/Mus_musculus.gene_info.202103.tsv`\n",
    "  - `data/dorothea_ABC_20210707_TF.sif`\n",
    "- Python libraries: `pandas`, `numpy`, `networkx`, `matplotlib`, `bonesis`.\n",
    "- `bash` and `clingo` are needed only if you choose to execute the optimization (ideally in WSL/Linux)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "study-example-001",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "study-guide"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "**Research question.** Chevalier et al. Case Study 1 asks which gene-regulatory subnetworks and Boolean rules can support six partially observed hematopoietic states, distinct terminal fixed points, and the specified reachability restrictions. This adaptation reduces the candidate regulatory domain using biological MSigDB seeds and data-driven neighbors *before* applying those constraints. It is therefore not a strict numerical reproduction of the paper's unrestricted component selection.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Three separate representations are used.** (1) A **state × gene** observation matrix (`0 = OFF`, `1 = ON`, `NaN = undetermined`). (2) A signed **regulator → target** prior network, whose edges say which regulations are permitted. (3) A **Boolean update function for each gene**, which is inferred later by synthesis. A candidate gene in the SIF is not yet an inferred logical rule.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "study-example-002",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": [
     "extended-explanation"
    ]
   },
   "source": [
    "### What the notebook receives and produces\n",
    "\n",
    "| Item | Example shape | Meaning | When consumed |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Binarized Nestorowa observations | 6 × many genes | Each state contains known OFF/ON values and missing values | State constraints and observed switching scores |\n",
    "| NCBI mouse gene-info TSV | alias mapping | Harmonizes gene symbols across sources | Before intersecting observations and regulation |\n",
    "| DoRothEA A–C SIF | `source`, `sign`, `target` | Permitted signed regulations; direction matters | Candidate network and BoNesis influence graph |\n",
    "| Mouse MSigDB GMT | many named sets × member genes | Biological annotations for prioritizing seeds, **not experimental proof** | Candidate preselection |\n",
    "| Reduced SIF | up to `MAX_CANDIDATE_GENES` nodes, potentially fewer after reloading | The actual signed domain presented to component optimization | `BoNesis(small_dorothea, small_observations)` |\n",
    "| Standalone `.sh` and Clingo log | generated script + solver output | Feasible choices of included nodes/strong constants; optimality depends on solver status | Subsequent component-answer ranking notebook |"
   ]
  },
